fined boundaries, with a named person accountable for evidence, rights, edits and the final decision. Treat workflow recommendations as editorial guidance, not proven performance outcomes. Do not assert traffic, rankings, savings, algorithm effects or search demand without approved evidence.\n\nSuggested structure\n1. Introduce the workflow and its human decision point.\n2. Choose an idea and write a bounded brief. Include the reusable template below.\n3. Establish an approved source set and record source versions. Explain how to track individual factual claims, quotations and citations against evidence; block unsupported claims and unresolved rights.\n4. Draft with AI within the brief and source set. Provide the illustrative drafting prompt below, making clear it is a starting point.\n5. Review the draft using the reviewer prompt below. A person checks evidence and rights, resolves findings and records the decision; an AI review does not replace that responsibility.\n6. Show the hypothetical tracker example, version chain and checklist. Explain that a change to the brief, evidence or draft can require review of dependent assets.\n7. Close with a practical next step: run one article through the process and keep the decision record.\n\nReusable brief template\n- Working title and article purpose:\n- Intended audience and what they already know:\n- Reader outcome and scope:\n- Required sections, examples and exclusions:\n- Approved source IDs, URLs, versions and permitted use:\n- Claims requiring verification and evidence owner:\n- Rights requirements and unresolved permissions:\n- Tone, format and editorial length target:\n- Acceptance criteria, reviewer and decision owner:\n- Brief version, date and links to dependent records:\n\nIllustrative drafting prompt\n\u201cUsing only the approved brief and source excerpts supplied below, draft the article for the specified audience. Stay within the stated scope. Identify each factual claim, quotation and citation with its source ID and precise supporting passage. Mark missing or ambiguous evidence as BLOCKED; do not invent facts, quotations or citations. Separate editorial recommendations and the hypothetical example from sourced claims. Return a draft and a claim-to-source list for human review. Brief: [version and text]. Approved source excerpts: [IDs, versions and excerpts].\u201d\n\nIllustrative reviewer prompt\n\u201cCompare this draft against brief [version] and source set [version]. List every factual claim, quotation and citation with the exact supporting passage, or flag it BLOCKED if support is missing, ambiguous or mismatched. Flag rights questions, scope drift and dependencies affected by changes. Do not assume a citation proves a claim. Return findings with locations and proposed remedies for a human reviewer. Draft: [version]. Source excerpts and rights records: [versions].\u201d\n\nHypothetical tracker example (invented for illustration, not a real project or result)\nA two-person team plans an article about a fictional bakery\u2019s bread-making process. The owner defines the scope and supplies material they are authorized to use. Row: claim C-01 | \u201cThe bakery begins its dough preparation each morning\u201d | source S-01, owner-provided process note v1 | evidence location: paragraph 2 | reviewer: editor | evidence status: pending verification | rights: pending confirmation | decision: blocked. Once the owner confirms the exact statement and permission, the editor records the evidence and rights decisions before considering release. If either remains unresolved, the claim stays blocked or is removed. This example makes no assertion about a real bakery.\n\nVersion and decision record\nPreserve identifiers or immutable snapshots and timestamps for the brief, source set and individual evidence, draft, review findings and resolutions, channel assets, package, and human decision. Link each item to the versions it depends on. Record who made each decision, what was approved or blocked, and why. Reopen affected checks when an upstream item changes; retain the prior decision history.\n\nChecklist for the article\n- The audience, outcome, scope, exclusions and acceptance criteria are explicit.\n- Every factual claim, quotation and citation has verified support in an approved, version-recorded source; unsupported material is removed or blocked.\n- Rights and permissions for all included material are documented or blocked.\n- A human has reviewed the draft and recorded the decision and unresolved findings.\n- Version links connect brief, sources, draft, review, channel assets, package and decision.\n- Hypothetical material is labeled; prompts do not imply that AI verification is sufficient.\n\nCandidate source instructions\nUse only the three supplied Google URLs for external factual claims. sources.json contains candidate claims and mappings, not evidence of support. An independent reviewer must fetch the actual pages, capture access date and source versions or snapshots, locate exact supporting passages, check qualifications and quotations, and reject or revise unsupported claims. Do not quote a page until its exact wording is verified. If a source is unavailable or does not support a statement, block or remove that statement. Source mapping does not establish rights clearance.\n\nDownstream package handoff\nAfter the canonical article is approved, prepare separate LinkedIn, X, Facebook, Instagram and Reddit drafts grounded in that article. Destinations are undecided; missing destination and current platform evidence block affected channels. Reddit remains blocked until a community and its current rules are supplied and checked. Prepare an original editable OpenFilm workflow diagram and a 1080 \u00d7 1080 PNG candidate for LinkedIn, using exact excerpts from the approved article and code-drawn shapes only, with no external images, music, footage or logos. Record rights and actual platform requirement checks and gaps; do not call the candidate approved. Assemble the article, channel drafts, editable project, rendered asset and review findings for a person. No publishing, scheduling, deployment, spending or credential creation is authorized. The canonical writer and independent reviewer must use the complete plan-seo-geo-growth package supplied to them by the runtime.\nEOF\ncat > /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-a32ef8ea00a06070ae036873eaaf2df1409f9b01df702f4f.scope/sources.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"C1\\\", \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Google advises creators to prioritize helpful, reliable content made for people.\\\", \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"S1\\\"]},\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"C2\\\", \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Google's guidance discusses the use of generative AI in creating content for Search.\\\", \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"S2\\\"]},\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"C3\\\", \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Google provides guidance on optimizing content for its AI search experiences.\\\", \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"S3\\\"]}\n  ],\n  \\\"sources\\\": [\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"S1\\\", \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content\\\", \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"EV-S1-PENDING\\\"},\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"S2\\\", \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/using-gen-ai-content\\\", \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"EV-S2-PENDING\\\"},\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"S3\\\", \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide\\\", \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"EV-S3-PENDING\\\"}\n  ]\n}\nEOF\nsha256sum /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-a32ef8ea00a06070ae036873eaaf2df1409f9b01df702f4f.scope/result.txt /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-a32ef8ea00a06070ae036873eaaf2df1409f9b01df702f4f.scope/sources.json\npython -m json.tool /var/home/user/Documents/ag